---

<SessionPage
  title="Session 9 · .NET: request record boundary trace"
  heading="Trace one JSON body by name, then follow one guard"
  lede="Read one supplied request record and Minimal API handler. Track three JSON values into named properties, then predict the rejected response. No project setup or code writing is needed."
  backHref="/topics/dotnet/"
  backLabel=".NET sessions"
>
  <SessionSection title="The path">
    <p><code>JSON body → TicketRequest request → named properties → Seats guard → HTTP response</code></p>
    <p>ASP.NET Core passes the body-bound value to the handler as <code>TicketRequest request</code>. The record gives that value three named properties: <code>EventCode</code>, <code>Seats</code>, and <code>Note</code>.</p>
  </SessionSection>

  <SessionSection title="Small model: match names, not order">
    <p>Suppose an object has properties named <code>Size</code> and <code>Color</code>. This JSON writes <code>Color</code> first:</p>
    <Code code={`{
  "Color": "blue",
  "Size": 3
}`} lang="json" />
    <ul>
      <li><code>Color</code> is <code>"blue"</code>.</li>
      <li><code>Size</code> is <code>3</code>.</li>
    </ul>
    <Callout>JSON object members match the named members. Their written order does not choose constructor positions.</Callout>
  </SessionSection>

  <SessionSection title="Supplied request record">
    <p>This record is supplied in <code>TicketRequest.cs</code>:</p>
    <Code code={recordCode} lang="csharp" />
  </SessionSection>

  <SessionSection title="Supplied Program.cs endpoint">
    <Code code={endpointCode} lang="csharp" />
    <p>The visible rule accepts from <code>1</code> to <code>6</code> seats. If the condition is true, the first <code>return</code> ends this request.</p>
  </SessionSection>

  <SessionSection title="One request body">
    <Code code={requestBody} lang="json" />
    <p>The members are deliberately written as <code>Note</code>, <code>Seats</code>, then <code>EventCode</code>. Trace their names. Do not rearrange them into constructor order.</p>
  </SessionSection>

  <p><strong>Today’s one action:</strong> trace only the supplied request. Stop when it reaches a <code>return</code>. Do not analyse the other path, write code, or run a project.</p>

  <ReplyTask id="learn:dotnet/009:q1" difficulty={3} copyText={replyText}>
    <ol>
      <li>Give the handler parameter name and type.</li>
      <li>Give the values of <code>request.EventCode</code>, <code>request.Seats</code>, and <code>request.Note</code>.</li>
      <li>In one short sentence, explain why the JSON member order does not move the values into constructor positions.</li>
      <li>Give the resulting HTTP status and JSON payload.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>Exact JSON spacing does not matter.</p>
  </ReplyTask>
</SessionPage>
